    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let's improve on the simple Predator-Prey model.
                There is a famous model called the Lotka-Volterra Predator-Prey
                model that we will build here.
            </p>

            <p>
                First, note that when the change of a state depends on two
                different states interacting with each other, we can model this
                by saying the derivative is jointly proportional to both states,
                i.e., the derivative is proportional to the product of the two
                states.
                This type of interaction might be considered an "encounter."
            </p>

            <p>
                If we take <m>x(t)</m> as the number of prey at time <m>t</m>
                and <m>y(t)</m> as the number of predators at time <m>t</m>,
                then we have the following assumptions on prey:
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        Left alone, the birth rate of the prey is proportional
                        to the population size of the prey.
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        The prey are more easily killed if there are many prey
                        and/or many predators, so the number of prey killed is
                        jointly proportional to the two populations (since we
                        are assuming the prey changes proportional to the number
                        of encounters between prey and predator).
                    </li>
                </ul>
                We have the following assumptions on predators:
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        The predator population grows proportionally to the
                        number of encounters between predator and prey (that is,
                        jointly proportional since predators have evolved to
                        reproduce when food is plentiful, but not as much when
                        food is scarce).
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        Predators can be very territorial, so the death/exit
                        rate is proportional to the number of predators.
                        (That is, the predators are more likely to be killed or
                        driven off as the predator population increases.)
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the system of differential equations (aka your
                    model)?
                    If it helps, first draw a schematic.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Assume you start with the following:
                    <ul>
                        <li>Initial prey is 1 (assume a scaled amount)</li>
                        <li>
                            Initial predators is 0.5 (assume a scaled amount
                            because you can't have half of a predator)
                        </li>

                        <li>The birth rate constant of prey is 0.7</li>
                        <li>
                            The constant defining the death rate of prey is 0.4
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            The constant defining the growth rate of predators
                            is 0.1
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            The constant defining the death rate of predators is
                            0.7
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                    Create code in Python and plot the solution.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What are some observations you can make from the plots of
                    the solution?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Change the growth rate of the predators to 0.08 and plot the
                    predators with the previous parameter values and the
                    predators with this new parameter value on the same graph.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What do you notice happened to the predators when you
                    decreased the parameter in part d from 0.1 to 0.08?
                    Anything interesting?
                    Why might it be interesting in context?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
